CATS=[('p1','Starting Primary 1'),('topics','Maths topics explained'),('home','Helping at home'),('strong','Strong and curious learners'),('mi','About Math Inquirers')]
QA=[
('p1','p1-maths','What maths will my child learn in Primary 1?',
 'Primary 1 maths covers numbers to 100, adding and subtracting, a first look at multiplication and division, shapes, length, time, money and picture graphs.',
 ['A typical order across the year (schools sequence topics a little differently):','<ul><li><b>Semester 1:</b> numbers to 10, adding and subtracting within 10, shapes, ordinal numbers (1st, 2nd, 3rd), numbers to 20, adding and subtracting within 20, picture graphs, numbers to 100.</li><li><b>Semester 2:</b> adding and subtracting within 100, length, multiplication, division, time and money.</li></ul>','Since the 2021 syllabus, P1 children also measure length in centimetres, tell time to 5 minutes and use a.m. and p.m., so these come up earlier than many parents remember.'],
 ['Radin Mas Primary, P1 Maths parent briefing 2024','https://www.radinmaspri.moe.edu.sg/files/Slides%20for%20Parents/2024/Mathematics_P1_2024.pdf']),
('p1','k2-ready','Is my K2 child ready for Primary 1 maths?',
 'Most children are ready if they can count objects to 20 reliably, read and write numbers to 20, and stay with a short task for 10 to 15 minutes.',
 ['Signs to look for:','<ul><li>Counts out 17 objects without skipping or double counting.</li><li>Says one more and one less than a number without starting from 1.</li><li>Splits 7 objects into two groups in more than one way.</li><li>Continues a simple pattern and can say the rule.</li></ul>','If a few of these are shaky, that is normal for K2. A few weeks of short, playful practice usually closes the gap. We offer a free 15-minute readiness check for K2 children before they join Math Inquirers.']),
('p1','holiday-before-p1','What can we do in the holidays before Primary 1?',
 'Keep it short, playful and hands-on: counting real objects, number bonds to 10, and talking about maths in daily life.',
 ['<ul><li>Count things you use every day: spoons, steps, buttons. Then group them in 2s and 5s.</li><li>Play "make 10": how many ways can 10 blocks be split into two groups?</li><li>Let your child pay for something small with coins and count the change together.</li><li>Practise writing numbers 0 to 20 neatly, especially ones that are often reversed, like 3, 7, 12 and 21.</li></ul>','Ten minutes a day is plenty. Stop while it is still fun.']),
('p1','count-to-100','My child can already count to 100. Is that enough?',
 'Counting aloud is a good start, but Primary 1 needs number sense: knowing what numbers mean and how they relate.',
 ['A child who can recite to 100 may still struggle to say what comes before 40, or which is bigger, 59 or 61. Check that your child can:','<ul><li>Count on from any number, such as from 47 to 53.</li><li>Count back, such as from 30 to 22.</li><li>Explain why 61 is more than 59 (6 tens is more than 5 tens).</li><li>Show 34 as 3 tens and 4 ones with objects or a drawing.</li></ul>']),
('topics','number-bonds','What are number bonds?',
 'A number bond shows how a number splits into two parts, for example 7 is 5 and 2, or 4 and 3. It is the foundation for adding and subtracting in Singapore maths.',
 ['Children usually draw a number bond as a circle for the whole joined to two circles for the parts. Learning all the bonds for 10 (1 and 9, 2 and 8, and so on) makes mental maths much faster later: 8 + 5 becomes 8 + 2 + 3 = 13.','At home, use 10 small objects and two cups. Hide some under one cup and ask how many are hidden.'],
 ['Radin Mas Primary, P1 Maths parent briefing 2024','https://www.radinmaspri.moe.edu.sg/files/Slides%20for%20Parents/2024/Mathematics_P1_2024.pdf']),
('topics','equals-sign','How do I explain what the equals sign means?',
 'Teach "=" as "is the same as", not "the answer comes next". Both sides must have the same value.',
 ['Many children think 4 + 4 = 8 + 1 is fine because "4 + 4 is 8". Ask: "Is 4 + 4 the same as 8 + 1?" Use a balance or two piles of objects to check. Questions like 5 + 3 = __ + 4 build this idea well.']),
('topics','skip-counting','How do I teach skip counting?',
 'Start with objects in groups, then move to a number line, then to saying the pattern.',
 ['<ol><li>Put buttons in groups of 2 and count the groups: 2, 4, 6, 8.</li><li>Hop along a number line in 2s, 5s or 10s and say each landing number.</li><li>Ask "What will come next? How do you know?" so your child spots the rule.</li></ol>','Skip counting leads straight into multiplication in Semester 2.']),
('topics','money-p1','How is money taught in Primary 1?',
 'Children learn the Singapore coins and notes, count amounts, and make the same amount in different ways.',
 ['A good home activity is "how many ways can you make 20 cents?" with real coins. Encourage your child to start with the biggest coin and work down, so no way is missed or repeated. Our Coin Combos lesson builds on exactly this idea.']),
('topics','picture-graphs','What is a picture graph?',
 'A picture graph shows data with small pictures, where each picture stands for one item (or later, more than one).',
 ['P1 children read picture graphs and answer questions like "how many more chose apple than banana?". Make one together: ask family members their favourite fruit and draw one fruit per vote.']),
('topics','working-backwards','What does "working backwards" mean in maths?',
 'It means starting from the end result and undoing each step to find the starting number.',
 ['Example: "I think of a number. I add 4 and get 11. What is my number?" Undo the "add 4" by taking away 4: 11 − 4 = 7. Check: 7 + 4 = 11. This strategy is common in challenging word problems later on, and P1 children can start with simple one-step versions.']),
('home','how-long','How long should we practise maths at home?',
 'For a Primary 1 child, 10 to 15 focused minutes most days works better than one long session a week.',
 ['Short sessions keep confidence high. Mix one quick skill (like number bonds) with one puzzle that needs thinking. If your child is tired or upset, stop and try again tomorrow.']),
('home','guessing','My child guesses answers instead of working them out. What can I do?',
 'Ask "How do you know?" every time, including when the answer is right. Children quickly learn that the reason matters.',
 ['Give objects or a drawing to check with, and praise the checking: "I like how you counted to make sure." If guessing continues, the question may be too hard, so step back to a smaller number.']),
('home','school-method','Should I teach the school method or my own way?',
 'Use the school method as the main way, and show your own way as a second method to compare.',
 ['Different methods can confuse a young child if introduced at the same time. Ask your child to show you how they learned it in school first. Comparing two methods later is a great thinking exercise.']),
('home','daily-maths','How can I fit maths into daily life?',
 'Talk maths during ordinary moments: shopping, cooking, travelling and playing.',
 ['<ul><li>At the shop: "Which is cheaper? By how much?"</li><li>In the kitchen: "We need 3 eggs for each cake. How many for 2 cakes?"</li><li>On the MRT: "How many stops to go? How many have we passed?"</li><li>With toys: sort them, count them, and make patterns.</li></ul>']),
('home','frustrated','My child gets upset when maths feels hard. How can I help?',
 'Make it safe to be stuck: struggling a little is how thinking grows.',
 ['Give a smaller step rather than the answer: "Can you try it with objects?" or "What would happen with a smaller number?" Share that you also get stuck sometimes. End on a question your child can do so they finish feeling capable.']),
('strong','too-easy','My child finds school maths too easy. What should we do next?',
 'Go deeper rather than faster: give puzzles that need reasoning, not just next year\'s topics.',
 ['Good stretch tasks ask a child to find every possible answer, explain why something works, or work backwards. For example: "Find all the ways to make 30 cents with 5, 10 and 20 cent coins." This uses ideas from approaches common in gifted education (depth, reasoning and explaining why) and extends thinking beyond the school syllabus without racing ahead of it.']),
('strong','gep-changes','What is happening to the GEP?',
 'MOE is discontinuing the Gifted Education Programme in its current form and starting a refreshed approach from 2027.',
 ['From what MOE has announced:','<ul><li>Higher-ability pupils will be identified through a single, standardised identification exercise in Primary 3. The first cohort went through it in August 2026, and pupils can also be identified at the end of each semester in P4 and P5.</li><li>Every primary school will offer school-based provisions, expected to benefit about 10% of each cohort.</li><li>Pupils who need more challenge can attend weekly after-school advanced modules at a designated centre near their school (15 primary schools), instead of transferring to a GEP school.</li></ul>','Check MOE\'s website for the latest details, as arrangements may be updated.'],
 ['MOE press release, 3 March 2026','https://www.moe.gov.sg/news/press-releases/20260303-learning-together-with-different-strengths-and-needs']),
('strong','olympiad-p1','Are maths olympiad problems suitable for Primary 1?',
 'Some are, if they are chosen for thinking rather than speed and your child enjoys them.',
 ['Good olympiad-style tasks for P1 involve patterns, logic clues, listing all possibilities and simple working backwards. Avoid timed competitions at this age; the goal is curiosity and persistence. Watch for signs your child is ready: enjoys puzzles, explains reasons, and keeps trying when stuck.']),
('strong','enrichment-vs-tuition','What is the difference between enrichment and tuition?',
 'Tuition usually supports school work and exams; enrichment extends thinking and interest beyond the school syllabus.',
 ['For a P1 child who is coping well, enrichment can keep maths interesting without extra drilling. For a child who is struggling with school topics, targeted support or tuition on those topics comes first.']),
('mi','who-for','Who is Math Inquirers for?',
 'Children aged 7-8 (builds on Primary 1 maths) who enjoy puzzles or find school maths easy, and want to think more deeply.',
 ['K2 children starting Primary 1 next year can join after a free 15-minute readiness check. Every lesson has three levels (Core, Think Harder and Challenge), so each child is stretched at the right level.']),
('mi','lesson','What happens in a Math Inquirers lesson?',
 'Each 90-minute lesson follows the four Inquirer steps: notice the pattern, test it with objects, check the answer, explain why.',
 ['A lesson starts with a warm-up game, then a hands-on exploration in pairs, a short teaching segment, workbook practice at three levels, a challenge puzzle and a talk time where children explain their thinking. The tutor stamps each child\'s Inquiry Journal at the end.']),
('mi','between-levels','What if my child is between levels?',
 'Take the free 30-minute diagnostic for the level that matches your child\'s school year, then use the result to decide. Each level spans three ages and overlaps the next, so there is room to move up or down. You have the final say.',
 ['Levels are named by age, for example Ages 8-10 (builds on Primary 3 maths). A score of 70% or more means enrol at that level. A score of 40-69% means enrol at that level (the tutor will note that support may help) or choose one level down. Under 40%, we recommend one level down. If the diagnostic was too easy (90% or more), your child may also try the level up.', 'You can change the level before lesson 1 at no cost, and the price is the same at every level. Ages 6-8 (builds on Primary 1 maths) is the lowest level: weaker learners there start with the free taster and a tutor chat.']),
('mi','online','Are the classes online?',
 'Yes. Math Inquirers is online first: live video lessons with the same group of up to 8 children, from your own home.',
 ['We post the printed workbook and Inquirer Kit (coins, notes, ten-frames, counters) to your home before the first lesson. Your child needs a laptop or tablet with a camera, on a table with space for the kit.']),
('mi','take-home','What does my child take home?',
 'A Math Inquirers workbook, the Inquirer Kit, an Inquiry Journal stamped after every lesson, and a certificate.',
 ['Parents also get a short note each week with what we did and one thing to try at home. There is nothing to buy.']),
('mi','price','How much does Math Inquirers cost?',
 'S$650 for 10 lessons of 90 minutes (S$65 a lesson), with the workbook and all materials included. Until Term 1 2027 ends (12 Mar 2027), families pay an introductory S$500 (S$50 a lesson).',
 ['Families who join in the first intake are founding families: they keep S$50 a lesson for all of 2027 if they enrol in every term. New families from Term 2 2027 pay S$65 a lesson, and everyone pays S$65 from January 2028.', 'There is no payment at registration: we confirm your child\'s group first, then send payment details.']),
('mi','refund','What if we need to cancel?',
 'You can cancel within 60 days of paying. Before the first lesson, we refund everything you have paid. After lessons start, we refund what you have paid for the lessons your child has not attended yet.',
 ['For example, with 7 of 10 lessons left you get 7/10 of the fee back. If you pay in instalments, any instalment not yet due is simply cancelled. The workbook and kit are yours to keep.', 'After 60 days, fees are not refundable. To cancel, message us on WhatsApp or email and we refund by PayNow.']),
]
